# Vocabulary this project does not use in its own prose, stored as salted digests.
#
# WHY IT IS INDIRECT, stated plainly because an unexplained hash file is worse than the thing it
# replaces. This list previously shipped as plaintext and then, for a while, did not ship at all: a
# file naming the words a reader would look for publishes its author's expectation of finding them,
# and every word on it occurs zero times in the tracked tree, so the only surviving instance of each
# was the line forbidding it. Keeping it out of the repository fixed that and broke something else --
# the gate then ran only on the machine that already had the list, and never in a fresh clone or in CI,
# which is the one environment where the tree becomes public. Digests get both properties at once.
#
# WHAT THIS IS NOT. It is not secrecy. Salted sha256 defeats pasting a digest into a search engine; it
# does not stop anyone who already suspects a word from confirming it in one line. That is the whole
# intended strength: the file stops BEING the list, it does not hide it.
#
# FORMAT  <sha256 of SALT + ":" + token>  <word|part>
#   word  matches a complete token only (short tokens collide inside ordinary English)
#   part  matches any substring, so a token glued to other characters cannot slip past
# SALT    sinkforge/prose/v1
#
# REBUILD  see tools/check_prose.sh; the generator takes one token per line on stdin and is four lines.
# One row is a nonsense sentinel the gate scans for on every run as its positive control, so a broken
# matcher fails loudly instead of reporting a clean tree.
